<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Hanning Speke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1827">1827</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Hanning Speke was a British army officer and explorer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. On Richard Burton's expedition he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> in 1858 and identified it as the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, a claim he set out to prove with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-augustus-grant">James Augustus Grant</a> in 1860–63, travelling through Karagwe and Buganda to the lake's outlet. He died in a shooting accident in 1864, on the eve of a public debate with Burton.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke.jpg" alt="John Hanning Speke" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Southwell Brothers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JH_Speke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Hanning Speke was born on 4 May 1827 at Orleigh Court, near Bideford in North Devon. In 1844 he was commissioned into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-army">Bengal Army</a> and served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> campaign and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a>. He became a lieutenant in 1850 and a captain in 1852, and spent his leave exploring the Himalayan Mountains. In 1854 he joined Richard Burton's expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, where a night attack by about 200 spearmen near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> left a fellow officer dead, Burton wounded through both cheeks and Speke captured and stabbed several times before he escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1857 Speke and Burton set out from Zanzibar to find the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> of the interior and the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. Both fell ill, and the daily work was done largely by porters, guides and soldiers. They reached Kazeh (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a>) in November and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> in February 1858. Burton was too weak to go further, so Speke went on alone with 34 men, led by Sidi Mubarak Bombay and Mabruki, and on 30 July 1858 became the first European to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>. He named it after the queen and judged from the boiling point of water that it stood about 4,000 feet above sea level.</p><p>The quarrel with Burton grew after their return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1859, when Speke announced the discovery publicly. Speke then led a second expedition with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-augustus-grant">James Augustus Grant</a>, leaving Portsmouth on 27 April 1860. On 28 July 1862 he reached the lake again and found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flowing out of it at the Ripon Falls, then spent time at the court of Muteesa I, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka of Buganda</a>. At Gondokoro in January 1863 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a> and later sent a telegram from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>: The Nile is settled. Burton objected that Speke had not followed the river all the way.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Speke and Grant reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in June 1863 and were welcomed as heroes, but disputes with Burton and with John Petherick weakened his standing. The Royal Geographical Society arranged a public debate with Burton at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> on 16 September 1864. On 15 September Speke died of a gunshot wound while shooting at Neston Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>. An inquest found the death accidental, while Burton spread the idea of suicide. He was buried at Dowlish Wake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>.</p><p>In 1874 to 1877 Henry Stanley sailed round the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> and showed that Speke had been right about the lake and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. Speke's account of East African peoples, in his Journal of the Discovery of the Source of the Nile (1863), described the physical features of African peoples and claimed to find among them a superior race.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Hanning Speke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This surplus of male labour helped Buganda build roads, palaces and fleets on a scale that impressed nineteenth-century European visitors such as <b>John Hanning Speke</b>, who arrived in 1862.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Zanzibar was also the starting point for European exploration of the interior: Richard Burton, <b>John Hanning Speke</b>, David Livingstone and Henry Morton Stanley all outfitted their expeditions in the town, often with the help of its merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>King Rumanyika I hosted <b>John Hanning Speke</b> and James Grant in 1861, and their accounts made Karagwe known in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-karagwe">Kingdom of Karagwe</a></li><li><blockquote>Explorers including Burton, <b>Speke</b>, Livingstone and Stanley stayed at Tabora.</blockquote><a href="/state/unyanyembe">Unyanyembe</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bideford, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Corsham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.02,-4.22&amp;year=1851" aria-label="Bideford on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Hanning Speke"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 5758h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 5758h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.02,-4.22&amp;year=1851">What was here in 1851? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
